meta {
  name: Save player row
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/players/:playerId
  body: formUrlEncoded
  auth: none
}

params:path {
  playerId: 1
}

body:form-urlencoded {
  run-power: 7.5
  ball-handling: 6
  ~is-goalie: on
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("returns the updated read-only row", function() {
    expect(res.getBody()).to.contain("<tr");
  });
}

docs {
  Saves the score edits and returns the read-only row.
  
  Enable the `is-goalie` field to mark the player a goalie. The handler
  uses `r.PostForm.Has("is-goalie")`, so the VALUE doesn't matter, only
  whether the key is present — mirroring how an unchecked HTML checkbox
  simply isn't submitted. Disable the field to clear the flag.
  
  ## Edge cases
  
  | Change | Expected |
  |---|---|
  | `run-power: 42` | "Run power needs to be number between 0-10." |
  | `ball-handling: -3` | matching ball handling message |
  | `run-power: kahdeksan` | same message — `ParseFloat` fails |
  | `ball-handling` empty | same message — an empty string isn't a valid float |
  | id `abc` in the URL | "Player ID needs to be number." |
  | id `999999` | see below |
  
  Both range and parse failures produce the same user-facing message, so
  from the outside you can't tell 42 from "kahdeksan". Fine in practice.
  
  The browser inputs carry min/max, so the range cases are only reachable
  from here.
  
  ## Two things to look at in the response
  
  **id `999999`.** The control flow in `handleSavePlayerRow` is tangled:
  the lookup treats `sql.ErrNoRows` as both a tolerated case and an error
  case, and if the final `UpdatePlayerAttributes` errors, the handler
  sets `GeneralError` but does NOT return — it falls through and renders
  the read-only row built from a zero-valued player. Send it and check
  whether the message survives into the response.
  
  **id `abc`.** The handler sets `GeneralError` and keeps going, querying
  player ID 0 before it reaches the check that stops it, so one bad
  request does a DB read it doesn't use.
}
